Global Dispatch | The Guardian


Editor's note

Last week, I attended the world=E2=80=99s biggest family planning conferenc=
e, ICPF 2025 in Bogot=C3=A1, Colombia. At one of the main sessions, Zambia,=
 Zimbabwe and the Democratic Republic of the Congo each
https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTN=
tZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHECViDoBXM111GPfwXhNil2De5_TPnoJhYA7ER3n0DVzukzmbSxnmmu4I_5=
epsEpIbLFppkmAaS06tG8kXdVDnj-J_LMuOTmpPg8jrd9LXVZBUWL4DcSI7S8IHmnD9pKg6TAOM=
6L9sAnhcDu_nG_iKKTDCEGjw1RfOpbz9oTXUu2CS4tlGLODBsErDSgNwh6bm1TbtoD88mQDlSB7=
-tLT7Ejv30SlJwws4oNbN1-4guOzMJgBhFfC7LLjcyBXwsaPAPOKuyZJpzmawVAtKXCVq3GNa8u=
tTaEdZKxnpJga5YohSrm3MdNVqHbrPcVdctpZtTh6LwMsAc8ZdQ8zu9R9fju/4uh/-sGAEIvxS5=
q29r7zaKWapQ/t0/h001.TvtY_2_HH3ZXt3wjwH2eoxtd9EsHUJw-ZkMyAXR0CTU
pledged to boost funding for reproductive health care
. Zambia=E2=80=99s minister of health, Elijah Julaki Muchima, described an =
increase in the country=E2=80=99s family planning budget from $4.5m (=C2=A3=
3.4m) in 2025 to $7.5m (=C2=A35.7m) in 2026 as =E2=80=9Ca historic step=E2=
=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cthe single most strategic investment in our nation=E2=
=80=99s human capital=E2=80=9D. UNFPA, which provides match funding, hailed=
 the commitments as proof that =E2=80=9Cacross the world, countries are pav=
ing a new path=E2=80=9D, after aid cuts.


That was the good news.


The bad news was that as low- and middle-income countries scramble to fill =
the gaping holes left by the dismantling of USAID and cuts from other donor=
 countries, there could be a lot more damage to come.


=E2=80=9CConsider the first nine months as round one,=E2=80=9D Beth Schlach=
ter, senior director of US external relations at MSI Reproductive Choices, =
told delegates. =E2=80=9CNow they=E2=80=99ve taken down the system of US fo=
reign assistance, the Trump administration is rebuilding it.=E2=80=9D


She was referring to new funding agreements that the US government is negot=
iating with individual countries. Little detail is available because these =
talks are happening behind closed doors with no input from civil society, b=
ut draft templates already show that the
https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTN=
tZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHECViDoBXM111GPfwXhNil2Dso1C3Kg_A6ALwAdWCIo8TRkHiF7AuixNjFd=
OQ03ud3EXEE_4swLXIEneBdkh1idL4hV09L_cElnXbmZNEnKfAWSaHVO4z4nYrOi8m_ERG4nvlj=
IQ3Ao_Zc1ePhYUtjKCPCZZ2TjdehSqd00M1DzgomVtRTS7RwVwRUlKPRGHKdOe4aK0TEYcqMmup=
__vqkHwbMUEnEvCM8SS2zA6FTOxOUH2wW3Fmuh_6aQ1A6AfjLHlRlegTFStqCic2uifrUHXJASa=
XJTmZog5Ld2yt7kWz4nCl87B1X86i3LAHsCXhyw/4uh/-sGAEIvxS5q29r7zaKWapQ/t1/h001.=
xIPp1oFJuEK-D9nrcl_ZQe6_Pb4z6Rd3HW9wX-WNed0
US expects countries to share data on infectious diseases
in return for aid, and the concern is that there will be more conditions th=
at limit family planning programmes, such as stipulations about the use of =
certain contraceptives, or abortion access.


Schlachter described such bilateral agreements as =E2=80=9Ca complete Troja=
n horse moment =E2=80=A6 You see a big shiny thing coming in but you don=E2=
=80=99t know there=E2=80=99s a beast in the belly.=E2=80=9D


However, these talks were not the only cause for concern. Since its launch =
in 2020, the anti-abortion Geneva consensus declaration (GCD) has been wide=
ly condemned by rights groups, yet it has been signed by 40 countries. Now,=
 Valerie Huber, an ex-Trump official and architect of the GCD, is leading e=
fforts to put the GCD=E2=80=99s principles into practice through the Proteg=
o project =E2=80=93 a development Jamie Vernaelde at reproductive justice o=
rganisation Ipas said should concern anyone working in family planning =E2=
=80=9Cas any policy based on GCD is catastrophic for bodily autonomy and re=
productive rights=E2=80=9D.


The summit made for a surreal experience. In the public spaces of the conve=
ntion centre, smiling stallholders handed out condoms with free coffee, and=
 young feminists made TikTok videos, while inside, warnings from experts wh=
o have spent years investigating anti-rights strategies felt chilling.


The conference was billed as a turning point for sexual and reproductive ri=
ghts. Which way it will turn is uncertain. But the biggest takeaway was tha=
t the 3,000-plus people who came to Bogot=C3=A1 are up for the fight. Or, a=
s the director of Family Planning 2030, Samukeliso Dube, put it: =E2=80=9CW=
omen can=E2=80=99t wait, the world can=E2=80=99t wait. We are not going to =
stop until we win.=E2=80=9D

Isabel Choat, commissioning editor, Global development


