<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">As a reporter living and working inside Ga=
za, I think one of the things that is difficult to understand from the outs=
ide is the scale of daily difficulties people face here.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Many of the conditions people in Gaza deal=
 with every day have become so deeply embedded in their lives they have sto=
pped seeing them as challenges =E2=80=93 they have become used to them.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">This is very difficult to explain to someo=
ne who is not living here. You can show the destruction, the tents and all =
this suffering, but it is much harder to show what happens to a person when=
 they experience these conditions for such a long time, what it feels like =
to live inside this, carrying all this fear and sadness. This is the realit=
y we wanted to capture when we documented the <a href=3D"https://ablink.edi=
torial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHE=
CViDoBXM111GPfwXhNil2DjMu4MP8IFqbssk2W7uY19f99GsjYgyDMYAf_C__-rkajG8TG37Yrl=
uneXkuTy0D5jq06rn3rKLn_WQNJy8q-an-UMu9IkeX9F18EwDk2eM2iFqJg8wg48OZJeiOMAYEG=
9hc7grh0lwr2NoCMwqeQMKbD8dx5vjLlTv0tTl_ajREqx-j1gUohOMLA5GzkXrmkk02g3ySmj_R=
wCQM5BjMzFaxTqxoMT-9SiH5Z4GO36C-CtHsoPr50h7YdvBSEBahcHrIbq6Lk4BC8V4NmOE0SsW=
poULlqCSHt2YTHTWildCs/4uh/-sGAEIvxS5q29r7zaKWapQ/h26/h001.ZQ8P51__WI4b-E7WX=
A2ylY67DmJx4CsyI3PZ2vOi6xM" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">lives of five ordinar=
y Palestinians living in Gaza</a> over the past week.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">We are now surprised by very basic things:=
 finding a staircase or a wall that we can lean against, or turning on a ta=
p and finding water. Walking into a place with electricity. Seeing a car th=
at is intact. Even seeing a proper bed has become something that catches ou=
r attention. It can feel strange to see a house that is still standing.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">On the other hand, things that we would no=
rmally be disgusted by have become part of the everyday. Insects, <a href=
=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18I=
RyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHECViDoBXM111GPfwXhNil2DjMu4MP8IFqbssk2W7uY19a3I0sWrPZC=
_pxHC13tEIxnI2l4xNSaw2C6K2WHTr0GO57tedAnlF-yygFN9X-fwikCMFXlhlZRvk6VvC5zMuy=
mLKwWE0A6hciiPvFN2vZ4acmAD-Dp2UpUETTRBKYdaT1I9sBWOdKKIg8cpYCyefRrXemMjxhFby=
g-rFqdXJ8mVvNBsQqxVLqr8n7VDAps2mSple_J4VLBNfTU6g_eYHPqwupeDdgX61yKo2D88is0b=
mC8QYLCSN9KwpbUzCypTAA/4uh/-sGAEIvxS5q29r7zaKWapQ/h27/h001.CE5GtQKThqELQ5_J=
jQj3CSAVm-QXPq44dCS4hjJ2BGE" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">rats, reptiles, flie=
s and mosquitoes</a> are everywhere, as is sewage flooding the streets. Peo=
ple have become used to cooking over a fire, <a href=3D"https://ablink.edit=
orial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEC=
ViDoBXM111GPfwXhNil2DjMu4MP8IFqbssk2W7uY19W1FemN1w45kVeWxetA_XmCLOOe3wrAHoE=
5c-cZqLB0U28nGHhbADNHbMQMTv8QBuutoe7E_JYCGhSj_m_KBEbbWesXYw4vPnqSg-rtbHznhK=
KwQR_a1uzAof1d5k1_PIs0hc5onUA0bHP9IjtdEdJ2b1h9vvPldnb_6r8utUFKwV3R9f2xsp8Hr=
TDQ5_AA9SxwUBa01bUSCYPDit-VhspbtoZkQvvefjZFRnAUFUSoUkPtMpmaT-AN5cWxgv9dEU3y=
F98ZLT4ikEx2t6Acluz5c3DXgQuDDjr9BAVgaOAJdjV5CVzAPwZXd9WxSeSnbrA/4uh/-sGAEIv=
xS5q29r7zaKWapQ/h28/h001.JTZ62r1oFqtT29FYxt32w2yvdQ2DHBPb5XofcY-3gQM" style=
=3D"color: #0077B6;">washing clothes by hand</a>, living without electricit=
y or running water, and seeing destruction everywhere. We live with all of =
it.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Children have become accustomed to things =
unimagined before. It has become normal to see <a href=3D"https://ablink.ed=
itorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneH=
ECViDoBXM111GPfwXhNil2DjMu4MP8IFqbssk2W7uY19UbAp1pA_9RVcZzD16d1VymBeZk2C1Nc=
eOdHqcI2hx4_1K3wK-sxprhUAgCt3yAPwcX_Ti9XaxpudRYJBfWyiqlTdD9NBkk2CmCEJWfz3NZ=
GWo1VaDr38ZVcFntF2Rd2hx5I-DfwslR6olwP9SOwyPteUqu39KoCkPLjjNPz2usSpekfM0niq2=
5-b3l9qcVbWOOykQ5RSrtuNZGCl4kAav2vCC3f38tyj7_7-Fd7Pv8SSGi-vIZk0yEyDALw8bAaL=
YOpkMGAZt9KTDUy5yh6v93glMLbi2SsqlVuwFGp4-aGt8RTewH9JjGPExjtAZnRppMoAq_R8xe0=
pKL1w4tPWVs/4uh/-sGAEIvxS5q29r7zaKWapQ/h29/h001.PRdBrY6KiTMXh2NZhSoOhV4tGbo=
FCmKXTtm-Jt5rNBQ" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">a child such as Ramadan</a>, ba=
refoot, forced to work in extreme heat, wearing worn-out clothes because he=
 has nothing else. And it has become strange to see a child clean and well =
dressed, going to school regularly.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">I think this is one of the saddest things =
about this stage of the war =E2=80=93 things that were once impossible to i=
magine have become a way of life, while normality can now feel unsettling a=
nd extraordinary.</p>
